\newpage
\begin{verbatim}
% TRANSFORMATION TOblPartHop
< m1: CLAUSE{CLAUSErec1}
            [ mu1,
              predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}
                     [ mu2, 
                       head/VERB{VERBrec1}[mu3],
                     I2:[
                         partrel/PART{PARTrec1},
                         I1:rel1/T1,
                         mu4
                       ]
                     ],
              mu5
            ]
>

< m : CLAUSE{CLAUSErec1}
            [ mu1,
              predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}
                     [ mu2, 
                       head/VERB{VERBrec1}[mu3],
                   I2:[
                       I1:rel1/T1,
                       partrel/PART{PARTrec1},
                       mu4
                      ]
                     ],
              mu5
            ]
>

   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       I1: T1.CAT IN [NP, NPVAR] WITH
           T1.REC.NPhead IN [persproNP,reflNP, itNP]
       I2: RIGHTTOLEFT
      >

      COMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: @
         >
      DECOMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: @
         >
&

\end{verbatim}
\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] TOblPartHop
\item[* relevant examples:] I gave him up, I don't need you handing me out that 
sort of advice, Hand it out {\em to\/} me, He turned himself in
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) English
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] XPPROPtoCLAUSE
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class) TC\_ParticleHop
\item[* date of creation:] 7-APR-1988 13:10:47  
\item[* author:] margreet sanders
\item[* copied from:] --
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: 3/5/89\ \ \ \ \ author: margreet sanders\\
Added condition on reflexives
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] To move the particle away from the verb, if the following 
argument (which may be an objrel but also an indobjrel if one can occur with a 
particle) is a personal pronoun or a reflexive (either an inherent reflexive or 
an argument reflexive).
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals)
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:]
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:]
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item dictionary:\\

\item rules:\\
It is assumed that CNVARs will always be subject to shiftrules, so there 
is no reason to have the particle hop over a CNVAR here while the VAR will move 
on later anyway.
\end{enumerate}
\item[\ \ consequences in the grammar:]
\item[\ \ translation aspects:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate} 
\item within this language:\\

\item in other languages:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[* relevant literature/documents:]
\item[\ \ remarks:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item problems:\\
\item modifications:\\

\end{enumerate}
\end{description}

\newpage
\begin{verbatim}
% TRANSFORMATION TOptPartHop
< m1: CLAUSE{CLAUSErec1}
            [ mu1,
              predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}
                     [ mu2, 
                       head/VERB{VERBrec1}[mu3],
                       partrel/PART{PARTrec1},
                       I1:rel1/T1,
                       mu4
                     ],
              mu5
            ]
>

< m : CLAUSE{CLAUSErec1}
            [ mu1,
              predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}
                     [ mu2, 
                       head/VERB{VERBrec1}[mu3],
                       I1:rel1/T1,
                       partrel/PART{PARTrec1},
                       mu4
                     ],
              mu5
            ]
>

   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       I1: T1.CAT IN [NP, NPVAR] WITH
             ( T1.REC.NPhead <> persproNP AND
               T1.REC.NPhead <> reflNP    AND
               T1.REC.NPhead <> itNP )
      >


      (* no COMP part *)

      DECOMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: @
         >
&

\end{verbatim}
\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] TOptPartHop
\item[* relevant examples:] I gave the boy up, I don't need you handing my 
children out that sort of advice, Hand the gun over {\em to\/} me
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) English
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] XPPROPtoCLAUSE
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class) TC\_ParticleHop
\item[* date of creation:] 20-APR-1988
\item[* author:] margreet sanders
\item[* copied from:] TOblPartHop
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] To put the particle back right after the verb, if in analysis it 
has hopped over a following NP. Note that heavy NPs will shift, so that 
malconstructions like `I called that woman whom we talked about last week up' 
are refused in surface position (if a condition on heaviness can be found).
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals)
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:] In generation, no hop 
over NPs is allowed (esp. since heaviness conditions are hard to formulate, and 
constructions without hop usually are acceptable).
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:]
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item dictionary:\\

\item rules:\\
\end{enumerate}
\item[\ \ consequences in the grammar:]
\item[\ \ translation aspects:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate} 
\item within this language:\\

\item in other languages:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[* relevant literature/documents:]
\item[\ \ remarks:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item problems:\\
\item modifications:\\

\end{enumerate}
